          </p>

          <h3>Training, without a fourth hurdle</h3>
          <p>
            Training was a separate product. When an employee fails a simulation, they're prompted to
            complete a set of multiple-choice questions the security team can fully customize. It came
            with its own settings, and it had to live inside the campaign builder. Our three-step flow
            was about to become four, and the under-a-minute goal was at risk.
          </p>
          <p>
            I worked through it with my PM using three levers: cut scope, reduce steps, and consolidate.
            Consolidation did the most work. After many rounds of iteration with my PM and head of
            design, we dropped the step-by-step wizard for a single form that reveals settings only when
            they need attention. Training became an optional toggle, there when you want it and out of
            the way when you don't.
          </p>
